<a2ui>
$/={comparison_data:[{signal:"El Niño",ocean_pattern:"Warmer central/eastern Pacific",effect:"Below-normal rainfall, more dry spells, higher heat risk",reason:"Weakens moisture flow and monsoon circulation"},{signal:"La Niña",ocean_pattern:"Cooler central/eastern Pacific",effect:"Above-normal rainfall more often",reason:"Strengthens circulation and moisture transport"},{signal:"Positive IOD",ocean_pattern:"West Indian Ocean warmer than east",effect:"Often supports stronger monsoon rain",reason:"Adds moisture and can counter El Niño"},{signal:"Negative IOD",ocean_pattern:"East Indian Ocean warmer than west",effect:"Often weakens monsoon rain",reason:"Reduces convection near India and can worsen El Niño’s impact"}]}
root=Column([a,b,c,f,i,p,v,ab],gap="md",padding=16)
a=Text("How these climate signals affect the Indian monsoon","h2")
b=Card([c])
c=Column([d,e],"sm")
d=Text("El Niño is the warm phase of ENSO in the tropical Pacific, and it usually suppresses the Indian summer monsoon by weakening large-scale circulation and moisture transport toward India.[4][10]",variant="body")
e=Text("La Niña is the cool phase of ENSO, and it usually strengthens the monsoon by supporting stronger monsoon circulation and moisture supply, which raises the chance of above-normal rainfall.",variant="body")
f=Card([g])
g=Column([h,i],"sm")
h=Text("The Indian Ocean Dipole (IOD) measures the west–east sea-surface temperature difference in the Indian Ocean; a positive IOD generally favors more rain over India, while a negative IOD tends to reduce monsoon rainfall.",variant="body")
i=Column([j,k],"sm")
j=Text("The key point is that ENSO and IOD interact: a positive IOD can partly or sometimes strongly offset El Niño’s drying effect, while a negative IOD can reinforce it.[8]",variant="body")
k=Table(columns=[{key:"signal",label:"Signal"},{key:"ocean_pattern",label:"Typical ocean pattern"},{key:"effect",label:"Usual effect on Indian monsoon"},{key:"reason",label:"Why it matters"}],statePath="/comparison_data",title="Comparison table",domain="comparison",preferredPresentation="cards",primaryColumn="signal")
l=Text("Historical examples","h2")
m=Card([n])
n=Column([o,p],"sm")
o=Text("1997: A strong El Niño occurred, but a positive IOD helped reduce its drying impact, so India did not experience the full expected monsoon failure.[5][6]",variant="body")
p=Card([q])
q=Column([r,s],"sm")
r=Text("2002 and 2009: These are widely cited El Niño drought years for India, showing the typical negative ENSO–monsoon link.",variant="body")
s=Text("2019: Research shows the monsoon was shaped by the combined influence of a strong positive IOD and an El Niño Modoki event, illustrating that multiple signals can act together rather than one signal deciding the outcome alone.[2]",variant="body")
t=Text("Why these signals cannot predict rainfall for one city with certainty","h2")
u=Card([v])
v=Column([w,x],"sm")
w=Text("These climate signals describe large-scale tendencies, not a city-level guarantee.",variant="body")
x=Text("India’s rainfall also depends on monsoon trough position, monsoon depressions, local topography, coastal circulation, and short-lived systems, which can make nearby places receive very different rainfall in the same season.[9]",variant="body")
y=Card([z])
z=Column([aa,ab],"sm")
aa=Text("Even during the same ENSO or IOD phase, rainfall can show a spatially uneven pattern across India, including regional wet–dry contrasts.",variant="body")
ab=Text("The relationship is also not perfectly stable: studies note that the ENSO–monsoon connection can weaken or vary over time, especially when IOD conditions co-occur.[15]",variant="body")
ac=Text("So these indicators can help estimate whether a season is more likely to be wet or dry, but they cannot forecast rainfall for one city with certainty.",variant="body")
ad=Text("Bottom line","h2")
ae=Card([af])
af=Column([ag,ah],"sm")
ag=Text("El Niño usually means weaker monsoon, La Niña usually means stronger monsoon, and the IOD can amplify or offset both.",variant="body")
ah=Text("Seasonal climate signals are useful for risk assessment, but not for exact city-level rainfall prediction.",variant="body")
</a2ui>